A||||3||DaveL||Circuit Board Curling Up||||1||
Z||000000||DaveL||02-02-2000||03:50 PM||||During an attempt tp repair a balky speedo, I removed the upper cover on my 87 SE 2.5 instrument cluster, I noticed that one of the dash light plugs is missing, and the right upper corner of the printed circuit board is curled up pretty good. Could this cause shorts in the system? I've been unable to locate a short to ground and an open in the temp sensor circuit. I think I can super glue the circuit board back down, but I'm wondering.<p>[This message has been edited by DaveL (edited 02-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||02-02-2000||07:21 PM||||I assume you mean that the copper trace has lifted from the base material. Adhesive is about the only repair you can do. How well "superglue" holds depends on what the base material is. Cyano Acrylates generally don't stick to plastic well.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaveL||02-03-2000||08:40 AM||||I mean to say that the plastic with the copper pattern on it has curled entirely away from the plastic that the whole circuit sits on, like a piece of paper that is rolled up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||02-03-2000||10:35 AM||||It's possible that one of the copper traces cracked because the plastic curled. If not, then use a little duct tape on the edges to hold it flat.||||reg||1
